author_final_remarks.value:
We sincerely thank all reviewers for their valuable and constructive feedback. We are grateful for your recognition of our work's key strengths:

1. **Innovative and Well-Motivated Approach**: We propose text-only training to teach a model vision-grounded decision-making, with a well-designed multi-stage GRPO framework and an adaptive reward;

2. **Cost-Efficient Training**: Our method offers practical, data-efficient benefits and directly addresses the scarcity of large-scale paired image–text datasets;
3. **Strong Empirical Performance**: Praxis-VLM consistently outperforms strong baselines across diverse benchmarks, demonstrating both its effectiveness and ability to generalize well, especially to out-of-domain tasks;

4. **Thorough and Thoughtful Analysis**: We provide comprehensive qualitative and quantitative analysis to evaluate the model's performance and reasoning quality.

We will incorporate additional results and clarifications into the final version. In particular:

1. Regarding our synthetic data creation: We have provided additional analysis on data diversity and clarified that it is scalable, efficient, and requires minimal manual effort. We will clarify this in the final revision;

2. Two reviewers expressed concerns about the base model. We have conducted additional experiments with Qwen2-VL to further demonstrate the robustness of our approach. We also conducted an ablation study regarding reward components to demonstrate the effectiveness of the length reward. We will include the additional results in the revision.

3. We have clarified that Praxis-VLM does not require generated image descriptions. It learns reasoning and decision-making solely from text-only training, without relying on multimodal data, and effectively transfers these abilities to multimodal scenarios during inference, directly addressing the data scarcity challenge. We will revise the paper to make this clearer.


We again express our gratitude for the time and effort you dedicated to our paper. To the best of our knowledge, our work is the first to explore leveraging language as the primary medium for instilling transferable reasoning and decision making skills in VLMs. We propose a novel text-based RL that learns high-level principles from language and grounds them in multimodal scenarios. Our method demonstrates strong adaptability across diverse benchmarks and holds practical value for real-world applications, especially when multimodal training data are scarce.

